    "created_at": "2026-11-02T19:51:51.9646474Z",
    "response": "It hath long been observed by the diligent gardener that the vegetable kingdom, when confined within the borders of cultivation, yields a prodigious variety of forms and hues far exceeding those found in the wild. A single species, once introduced from the forest into the cool, sheltered beds of the nurseryman's green house, soon begins to exhibit deviations from its parent stock; this is not due to any inherent instability within nature, but rather to the relentless pressure of artificial choice. When a particular flower displays a hue more intense than its kin, or a stem that bears fruit with greater copiousness, it becomes the object of special care. The gardener, acting as an agent of destiny, gathers seeds solely from those superior specimens and sows them in abundance, while neglecting the commoner varieties to let them fade into oblivion. Through this repeated operation of selection, carried out year by year with unwavering constancy, the offspring gradually assimilate the desired traits until they become so distinct as to appear almost alien to their ancestral lineage. Thus, a plant may be transformed from a humble wild type into a magnificent garden monster, its petals expanding beyond belief and its color burning like fire against the grey sky. This process demonstrates that while nature provides the raw material of diversity through mutation and chance, it is human will that directs the trajectory of these changes. The power to command the growth of life lies not in the magic of alchemy nor the prayers of the priest, but in the steady, methodical application of reason and observation by those who understand that every generation brings them closer to their chosen end.",
